WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
Block two.

2
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.550
Plot first, test second; getting data from somewhere; and then live coding.

3
00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:13.875
On the page, this block is the sections "Seeing the data", "Asking quantitative questions" and "Data coming from somewhere".

4
00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:23.450
The first half of this block is about statistics you can get wrong without any error at all, and about the libraries that already contain the method you were about to write.

5
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:27.700
Then I will write some code in front of you and get it wrong three times.

6
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.750
This is my favourite slide in the session, because the answer flips.

7
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.900
The question: in this dataset, is fold change related to expression level?

8
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.900
Do highly expressed genes change more, or less, than lowly expressed ones?

9
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.350
Compute Pearson's correlation on the raw numbers.

10
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:51.350
r equals minus nought point zero one, p equals nought point four three.

11
00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.925
No relationship whatsoever.

12
00:00:53.925 --> 00:00:57.700
Now plot it, with expression on a logarithmic axis.

13
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.975
And there is an obvious relationship: the lowly expressed genes change much more.

14
00:01:02.975 --> 00:01:04.950
So which is right?

15
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.200
The plot.

16
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.800
Let us see why the number lied.

17
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:15.675
Pearson's correlation measures a linear relationship, and expression levels span several orders of magnitude.

18
00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:22.450
Albumin, in liver, has a base mean of nine hundred and fifty-four thousand, nine hundred and sixty-four.

19
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:31.800
A handful of genes like that are so far from everything else that they dominate the calculation, and the pattern among the other three thousand becomes invisible.

20
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.975
Do it properly and the relationship appears.

21
00:01:34.975 --> 00:01:39.750
Pearson on the logarithm of expression: minus nought point three zero.

22
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:50.100
Spearman, which uses ranks and therefore does not care about the scale at all: minus nought point four two, with a p-value of two times ten to the minus one hundred and twenty-nine.

23
00:01:50.100 --> 00:02:01.575
And the biology behind it, which makes the result believable: a lowly expressed gene is measured from few reads, so its estimated fold change is noisier, and noise makes things look bigger.

24
00:02:01.575 --> 00:02:04.200
That is Session eight's subject.

25
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.050
The rule: plot first, test second.

26
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:13.500
If you had only run the test, you would have reported no relationship, with a perfectly correct p-value.

27
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.175
A short slide with a simple message: look for the function before you write it.

28
00:02:19.175 --> 00:02:21.750
SciPy has the statistics.

29
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:28.600
The Welch t-test from Session one is stats dot ttest underscore ind, with equal-var set to false.

30
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:37.475
On Session one's control and treatment numbers it gives t about minus eleven point nine and p about nought point zero zero zero one.

31
00:02:37.475 --> 00:02:47.200
Note the default is equal-var true, which is Student's t-test and assumes equal variances; Welch is usually the safer choice, and you have to ask for it.

32
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:55.650
SciPy also has hierarchical clustering: cluster dot hierarchy dot linkage, which repeatedly merges the closest clusters.

33
00:02:55.650 --> 00:03:01.575
That is the algorithm behind Session four's guide tree for multiple alignment, already written.

34
00:03:01.575 --> 00:03:08.150
And scikit-learn has the machine learning: linear regression, multilayer perceptrons, and everything else.

35
00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:14.775
In Sessions seven to nine you will build several of those by hand, once, because you should know what is inside.

36
00:03:14.775 --> 00:03:17.300
After that, call the library.

37
00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.850
The reason to prefer the library is not laziness.

38
00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:30.175
It is that these implementations handle the numerical edge cases, have been tested by thousands of people, and are what your reader will assume you used.

39
00:03:30.775 --> 00:03:33.125
Where does data come from?

40
00:03:33.125 --> 00:03:36.825
Four sources, and one pattern that covers all of them.

41
00:03:36.825 --> 00:03:38.825
A file on disk.

42
00:03:38.825 --> 00:03:40.825
A compressed file.

43
00:03:40.825 --> 00:03:42.300
An archive.

44
00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.925
And a database over the network.

45
00:03:44.925 --> 00:03:54.375
The pattern: every one of them gives you something Python calls a handle, which is an object you can read from, and SeqIO dot parse reads records from a handle.

46
00:03:54.375 --> 00:03:59.075
The handle is where the differences live; the reading code is identical.

47
00:03:59.075 --> 00:04:05.700
So a gzipped FASTA does not need unpacking first: gzip dot open gives you a handle.

48
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:12.025
A zip or tar archive opens with the zipfile or tarfile module, which gives you a handle per member.

49
00:04:12.025 --> 00:04:15.675
And NCBI gives you a handle over the network.

50
00:04:15.675 --> 00:04:23.525
Entrez dot efetch, with the database, the accession, and the format you want, inside a with statement, and then SeqIO dot read.

51
00:04:23.525 --> 00:04:35.225
Fetch N M underscore zero zero zero five one eight point five that way and you get Homo sapiens haemoglobin subunit beta, mRNA, six hundred and twenty-eight nucleotides.

52
00:04:35.225 --> 00:04:38.500
That is the call the live coding starts from.

53
00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:45.275
Three rules for using NCBI from code, because they are a shared resource and they will block you.

54
00:04:45.275 --> 00:04:48.700
Set Entrez dot email to your real address.

55
00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:56.550
It is required, and it means that if your script misbehaves they can tell you rather than just banning your institution's address range.

56
00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:00.750
Fetch many records in one request rather than many requests.

57
00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:07.250
Join your accessions with commas and pass them as one id argument: fifty accessions, one request.

58
00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:14.225
Tempting as it is to write a loop, a loop of fifty single requests is fifty times the load for the same data.

59
00:05:14.225 --> 00:05:19.150
And without an API key you are allowed roughly three requests per second.

60
00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:26.375
A tight loop will exceed that in milliseconds, and then you are blocked, and the error you get will not obviously say so.

61
00:05:26.375 --> 00:05:35.000
This generalises beyond NCBI: when you use somebody else's service from a script, find out what the limits are before you write the loop.

62
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.750
Now the live coding.

63
00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:44.600
I will type this in front of you rather than show finished code, because the point is the process and not the result.

64
00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:52.375
The task is small: fetch six globin genes, tabulate some properties, plot one against another, and work out the protein lengths.

65
00:05:52.375 --> 00:05:56.700
And I am going to make three mistakes, deliberately, one of each kind.

66
00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.850
I will not tell you when.

67
00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:01.300
Your job is to notice.

68
00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:07.350
If you are watching the recording, you are missing the typing, which is the part that does not record well.

69
00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:15.225
What follows is what happens and what you should take from it, and the script itself is on the teachers page, so you can run it yourself.

70
00:06:15.825 --> 00:06:19.325
First, the taxonomy, so you know what to watch for.

71
00:06:19.325 --> 00:06:21.025
Three kinds.

72
00:06:21.025 --> 00:06:23.450
Kind one: a Python error.

73
00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.225
The code stops, you get a traceback.

74
00:06:26.225 --> 00:06:28.725
This is the friendly kind.

75
00:06:28.725 --> 00:06:33.250
Something is wrong and you are told immediately, at the place it happened.

76
00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:35.900
Kind two: a wrong analysis.

77
00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:40.575
The code runs perfectly, and computes something other than what you meant.

78
00:06:40.575 --> 00:06:42.900
No error, no warning.

79
00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:48.050
The axes are swapped, or you filtered on the wrong column, or you compared the wrong groups.

80
00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.175
Kind three: biologically nonsensical.

81
00:06:51.175 --> 00:06:56.625
The code runs, the analysis is what you intended, and the result is impossible.

82
00:06:56.625 --> 00:06:59.375
Only knowing the biology tells you.

83
00:06:59.375 --> 00:07:01.850
Watch for all three as I type.

84
00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:05.725
And afterwards, ask yourself which was hardest to notice.

85
00:07:05.725 --> 00:07:09.600
The answer should be two and three, because nothing complained.

86
00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.200
That ranking is the whole reason this session exists.

87
00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.800
Our tools are excellent at catching kind one and completely blind to kinds two and three.

88
00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:22.200
Here is the plan, in four steps.

89
00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:32.700
Fetch six globins in one Entrez request: HBB and HBA1 and HBA2, the haemoglobin chains; myoglobin; neuroglobin; and cytoglobin.

90
00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:38.200
Tabulate them into a DataFrame: gene name, mRNA length, GC content.

91
00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:45.875
Plot GC content against mRNA length, with length on the x-axis, because that is how the question was phrased.

92
00:07:45.875 --> 00:07:49.150
And then compute the protein length for each gene.

93
00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.350
Now, the three mistakes, which I will walk through.

94
00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:55.100
Mistake one is a Python error.

95
00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.650
I type genes, square bracket, g c, dot mean.

96
00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.400
KeyError: g c.

97
00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:06.550
The rule from Block three: read the last line of a traceback first, because that is what went wrong.

98
00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:10.925
A KeyError on a DataFrame means there is no column with that name.

99
00:08:10.925 --> 00:08:16.825
So I type genes dot columns and look: the column is called g c underscore content.

100
00:08:16.825 --> 00:08:19.075
Fixed in ten seconds.

101
00:08:19.075 --> 00:08:23.425
Mistake two is a wrong analysis, and it is the interesting one.

102
00:08:23.425 --> 00:08:28.550
The question was GC against length, so length belongs on the x-axis.

103
00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:36.800
I type plt dot scatter with g c underscore content first and length second, and label the x-axis mRNA length.

104
00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.375
It runs.

105
00:08:38.375 --> 00:08:41.825
The plot appears and looks entirely reasonable.

106
00:08:41.825 --> 00:08:45.400
Mistake three is biologically nonsensical.

107
00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:51.250
I compute protein length as mRNA length divided by three, using integer division.

108
00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:52.825
It runs.

109
00:08:52.825 --> 00:08:59.650
Cytoglobin comes out as six hundred and fifty-four amino acids and HBB as two hundred and nine.

110
00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:02.925
Each of the next three slides takes one of those.

111
00:09:03.525 --> 00:09:09.400
Here is the plot from mistake two, and the method for catching it, which is the transferable part.

112
00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.400
The plot looks fine.

113
00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:15.175
Points scattered, axes labelled, nothing suspicious.

114
00:09:15.175 --> 00:09:17.425
So how do you catch it?

115
00:09:17.425 --> 00:09:20.675
Find one point you already know the answer for.

116
00:09:20.675 --> 00:09:24.450
Cytoglobin has the longest mRNA of these six.

117
00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:29.325
So if length is on the x-axis, cytoglobin should be the furthest right.

118
00:09:29.325 --> 00:09:30.475
Is it?

119
00:09:30.475 --> 00:09:33.675
Then read the tick labels on the x-axis.

120
00:09:33.675 --> 00:09:37.650
They run from about nought point five to nought point six five.

121
00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:41.325
Lengths are hundreds or thousands of nucleotides.

122
00:09:41.325 --> 00:09:46.775
Nought point five to nought point six five is GC content, which is a fraction.

123
00:09:46.775 --> 00:09:51.800
So the axis is GC content and the label says mRNA length.

124
00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:57.025
The arguments are the wrong way round, and the label is a lie I wrote myself.

125
00:09:57.025 --> 00:09:59.875
Nothing in Python could have caught that.

126
00:09:59.875 --> 00:10:06.750
The function was given two columns of numbers and asked to plot one against the other, and it did exactly that.

127
00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:13.275
The habit that catches it: find a point whose position you can predict, and check that it is where you expect.

128
00:10:13.275 --> 00:10:17.900
That takes five seconds and it works on every plot you will ever make.

129
00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:25.250
And mistake three, which is my favourite, because it is invisible to everything except knowledge of biology.

130
00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:29.350
Protein length equals mRNA length divided by three.

131
00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:33.425
That is correct arithmetic: three bases per codon.

132
00:10:33.425 --> 00:10:35.250
The code runs.

133
00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:38.425
The numbers are plausible-looking integers.

134
00:10:38.425 --> 00:10:42.500
Cytoglobin: six hundred and fifty-four amino acids.

135
00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:45.275
HBB: two hundred and nine.

136
00:10:45.275 --> 00:10:47.325
Now, is that wrong?

137
00:10:47.325 --> 00:10:49.775
Nothing in the data says so.

138
00:10:49.775 --> 00:10:52.125
The arithmetic is right.

139
00:10:52.125 --> 00:10:54.175
The code is right.

140
00:10:54.175 --> 00:10:58.050
But globins are about a hundred and fifty amino acids.

141
00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:00.825
All of them, across the whole family.

142
00:11:00.825 --> 00:11:05.000
A six-hundred-and-fifty-four-residue globin is not a thing.

143
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:10.525
And the cause is the slide from Block one: the mRNA is not the coding sequence.

144
00:11:10.525 --> 00:11:16.375
It has untranslated regions at both ends, and those are not translated into anything.

145
00:11:16.375 --> 00:11:22.100
The fix is to use the CDS feature from the record, exactly as we saw earlier.

146
00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.975
I want to be clear about what caught this.

147
00:11:24.975 --> 00:11:27.875
Not the computer, and not the data.

148
00:11:27.875 --> 00:11:32.325
Domain knowledge caught it: knowing roughly how big a globin is.

149
00:11:32.325 --> 00:11:40.800
Which is an argument for knowing your biology, and also an argument for always asking, of any number you compute, whether it is the right size.

150
00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:46.825
And here are the corrected numbers, which are themselves a small biological result.

151
00:11:46.825 --> 00:11:52.550
The proteins are between a hundred and forty-two and a hundred and ninety amino acids.

152
00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:54.975
Globins, as expected.

153
00:11:54.975 --> 00:12:02.075
The mRNAs are between five hundred and seventy-six and one thousand nine hundred and sixty-two nucleotides.

154
00:12:02.075 --> 00:12:09.675
So the untranslated regions are between a hundred and forty-seven and one thousand three hundred and eighty-nine nucleotides.

155
00:12:09.675 --> 00:12:14.950
In the extreme case, the untranslated part is much longer than the coding part.

156
00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:20.775
Which tells you something worth knowing: mRNA length says very little about protein length.

157
00:12:20.775 --> 00:12:28.400
The variation between these six transcripts is mostly variation in their untranslated regions, not in their proteins.

158
00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:31.800
One last note, which is a Session eight preview.

159
00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:43.875
With six genes, the correlation between mRNA length and protein length is r equals nought point seven eight, which sounds strong, but p equals nought point zero seven, which is not significant.

160
00:12:43.875 --> 00:12:46.275
Six points is not many.

161
00:12:46.275 --> 00:12:49.125
That is the next discussion problem.

